  "activity": {
    "id": "retrograde_motion_activity",
    "name": "Understanding Apparent Retrograde Motion of Mars",
    "description": "A comprehensive AR-guided exploration of why outer planets like Mars appear to move backward across the night sky. Students progress through observing retrograde motion from Earth's perspective, learning geocentric epicycle explanations, examining the heliocentric model, linking both viewpoints, conducting embodied orbital simulations, comparing outer planets, predicting retrograde configurations, and reflecting on key concepts. This activity builds understanding from phenomenological observation to physical explanation and predictive reasoning.",
    "language": "en",
    "workplace": "astronomy_retrograde_workplace",
    "start": "step_1_night_sky_observation",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_1_night_sky_observation",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "hololens_unit_1",
        "location": "night_sky_workstation",
        "predicate": "narration_retrograde_intro",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Retrograde Puzzle: Observing Mars in the Night Sky",
          "description": "Welcome to the retrograde motion laboratory. You are standing on a virtual Earth at night, looking up at the starfield of the ecliptic plane. The constellations Leo, Virgo, Libra, and Scorpio are labeled around you. Mars appears as a distinct red point against the stars. Watch closely as we fast-forward through time over six months. Notice how Mars's position changes: it drifts slowly eastward against the background stars for two months, then slows, halts, and moves westward for about two months, tracing a loop of roughly 15 degrees. Finally, it slows again and resumes eastward motion. A trail marks the complete path. This backward-motion puzzle drove centuries of astronomical debate. Why does a planet move backward? Let's solve this mystery."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "night_sky_workstation",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "starfield_background"
            },
            {
              "target": "night_sky_workstation",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "constellation_labels"
            },
            {
              "target": "mars_object",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "mars_retrograde_trail"
            },
            {
              "target": "night_sky_workstation",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "mars_position_indicator",
              "poi": "leo_constellation"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [
            {
              "target": "astronomy_student",
              "type": "person",
              "text": "Focus on the starfield and watch Mars trace its path through time."
            }
          ],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": true,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "night_sky_workstation",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "voice",
            "id": "step_1_proceed",
            "type": "action",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_2_geocentric_model",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "hololens_unit_1",
        "location": "geocentric_workstation",
        "predicate": "narration_geocentric",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Geocentric Explanation: Epicycles and Deferents",
          "description": "Now we explore how ancient astronomers explained retrograde motion without moving the Earth. In the geocentric model, Earth sits at the center. Mars rides on a small circle called the epicycle (labeled here) that itself orbits on a larger circle called the deferent. Both circles are centered on Earth. Watch as this mechanical system runs: when Mars reaches the inner part of its epicycle\u2014the side closest to Earth\u2014the combination of its motion on both circles makes it appear to move backward as seen from Earth. The model does produce the observed loop! However, careful observations revealed a problem: to match increasingly accurate data, astronomers had to stack more and more epicycles on top of each other, making the system ever more complex and unwieldy. This complexity eventually led scientists to ask: could there be a simpler explanation?"
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "geocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "epicycle_deferent_animation"
            },
            {
              "target": "geocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "epicycle_label_text",
              "poi": "epicycle_label"
            },
            {
              "target": "geocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "deferent_label_text",
              "poi": "deferent_label"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [
            {
              "target": "astronomy_student",
              "type": "person",
              "text": "Observe how the epicycle motion produces the apparent backward motion."
            }
          ],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": true,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "geocentric_workstation",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "voice",
            "id": "step_2_proceed",
            "type": "action",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_3_heliocentric_view",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "hololens_unit_1",
        "location": "heliocentric_workstation",
        "predicate": "narration_heliocentric",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Heliocentric Solution: Simple Orbital Mechanics",
          "description": "Here is the heliocentric model\u2014a fundamentally different perspective. The Sun sits at the center. Earth orbits at 1 AU with a period of one year, while Mars orbits at 1.52 AU with a period of 1.88 years. Notice that Earth is closer to the Sun and orbits faster. Watch the two planets run their orbits at correct relative speeds. Roughly every 26 months, Earth catches up to and overtakes Mars on its inner, faster orbit. When this happens, the retrograde event that we observed from Earth unfolds naturally here\u2014no epicycles needed. The backward loop is not missing; rather, it is a consequence of relative motion: as Earth passes Mars on the inside, Mars appears to move backward against the distant stars. This is the same effect that makes a slower car appear to drift backward when your car passes it on the highway. The heliocentric model is simpler, requires no epicycles, and explains the phenomenon elegantly."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "heliocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "sun_object",
              "poi": "sun_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "heliocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "earth_planet_model",
              "poi": "earth_orbit_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "heliocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "mars_planet_model",
              "poi": "mars_orbit_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "heliocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "heliocentric_orbital_animation"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [
            {
              "target": "astronomy_student",
              "type": "person",
              "text": "Watch Earth overtake Mars. Notice the simplicity compared to the epicycle model."
            }
          ],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": true,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "heliocentric_workstation",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "voice",
            "id": "step_3_proceed",
            "type": "action",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_4_perspective_linkage",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "hololens_unit_1",
        "location": "heliocentric_workstation",
        "predicate": "narration_perspective_link",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Linking Two Perspectives: From Orbit to Sky",
          "description": "Now we connect the heliocentric orbits directly to the night-sky observations. In this side-by-side view, the left panel shows the heliocentric model; the right panel shows what an observer on Earth sees. A line of sight is drawn from Earth, through Mars, and extended out to the background starfield. As the animation runs, watch this line of sight sweep across the constellations. During normal motion (Earth approaching Mars), the line sweeps eastward. But at the moment Earth begins to overtake Mars, the line reverses direction and sweeps westward\u2014and simultaneously, in the right panel, Mars's apparent position retraces its path against the stars. The retrograde loop is real, produced by the geometry of the two orbits, not by any flaw in perception or measurement. This linkage shows that one physical reality\u2014two planets on smooth orbits\u2014produces two different observational patterns depending on your vantage point."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "heliocentric_workstation",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "side_by_side_view"
            },
            {
              "target": "heliocentric_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "line_of_sight_indicator"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [
            {
              "target": "astronomy_student",
              "type": "person",
              "text": "Observe how the line of sight tells the complete story of retrograde motion."
            }
          ],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": true,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "heliocentric_workstation",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "voice",
